WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.850
The Doppler effect. In Sheldon's words,

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:06.529
"it's the apparent change in the
frequency of a wave caused by relative

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:10.840
motion between the source of the wave and the observer".

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.760
The Doppler effect is perhaps best explained visually.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.090
So here's a thing that is emitting
waves.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:20.570
It could be a fire truck emitting
sound, it could be a star emitting light,

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.810
it could be a duck creating ripples on a
pond.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:27.070
Those are all waves, and they all look something like this. We see the Doppler effect

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:27.890
happening

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:32.260
when the thing that is emitting waves
moves. In the direction it's moving,

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.489
the wavefronts bunch up, and behind it,
they spread out.

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:39.080
If our object is moving towards a
stationary observer,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.110
these bunched up waves are observed at a high frequency,

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.930
and if the object is moving away from a
stationary observer, the waves are ob-

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:46.350
served

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:51.089
at a lower frequency. So that is the Doppler effect.

00:00:51.089 --> 00:00:54.710
The apparent change in the frequency of
a wave caused by relative motion between

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:55.979
the source of the wave

00:00:55.979 --> 00:01:00.129
and the observer. It makes sense. But it
gets interesting when you consider some

00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:04.420
its applications. So let's say you are
standing in the middle of the road –

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.130
that's – that's you – and a car drives past you very fast.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.890
As it does so, it honks its horn, because
you're standing in the middle of the road.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:16.729
The horn to you might sound something
like this.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:20.049
So it starts at a high pitch and moves
to a lower pitch,

00:01:20.049 --> 00:01:22.469
even though from the driver's
perspective, the horn is playing the

00:01:22.469 --> 00:01:23.990
same pitch the entire time.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:27.820
So what's going on? As the vehicle's
coming towards you,

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.960
the sound waves that it's emitting bunch up, and so are delivered to you at a

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:36.450
higher frequency, which you interpret as a
higher pitch, because the frequency of

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.200
sound waves

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.240
is pitch, and then when the vehicle
passes you and is moving away from you,

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.509
the sound waves spread out, and so you
hear them

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:48.860
at a lower frequency – a lower pitch.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:52.570
So that's how the Doppler effect works
with sound. It also affects another kind of

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:53.090
wave –

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.380
light. So let's say you look out your
observatory and you see a star.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:01.340
Just like the car's sound waves, if the
star is moving towards you, even just a

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:01.970
little bit,

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:05.860
the light waves that it emits will be
bunched up, meaning that you see the

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.509
light at a higher frequency than it
actually is.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.140
Frequency in sound is pitch, so what does it mean for light?

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.550
Well, if we look at our Handy Pocket
Electromagnetic Spectrum Chart,

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:19.209
we'll see that a small change in
frequency for visible light will change its

00:02:19.209 --> 00:02:19.780
colour.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:24.180
Higher-frequency light waves means bluer light, and lower-frequency light waves

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.110
means redder light.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:29.239
This is called redshift, and it may possibly
be among the weirdest and coolest things

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:30.070
of all time.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:33.209
Stars, or anything that you can see,

00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:36.550
change colour depending on their
relative motion to you.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.970
Of course, you can't see this minute
difference with your eyes, but

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:43.959
astronomers, with the right equipment, can
use this effect to tell whether stars are moving

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:44.709
away from,

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:49.310
or towards, Earth. As it turns out, almost
everything we can see in the universe is

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:51.190
moving away from us very quickly,

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:54.769
which is both an important piece of
evidence for the Big Bang and an indication that

00:02:54.769 --> 00:02:57.890
the Earth might get very lonely in the
distant, distant, future.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:01.670
Thanks for watching this video. Let me
know if you got something out of it.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:02.019
Bye.

